WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.902
-Our first guest tonight
is a talented actor

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:04.971
and Grammy Award-winning
hip-hop legend.

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:07.173
He stars in "Law &amp; Order:
Special Victims Unit,"

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.409
now in its 24th season.

00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:12.144
You can see new episodes
Thursday nights here on NBC

00:00:12.144 --> 00:00:12.979
and streaming
the next day on Peacock.

00:00:12.979 --> 00:00:14.380
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.683
-And by the way,
I got something for you.

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:18.718
-Hmm?

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:32.031
A bullet?

00:00:32.031 --> 00:00:35.768
-Same caliber as the one
you got shot with.

00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:38.805
You have to own your trauma,
or your trauma will own you.

00:00:38.805 --> 00:00:40.006
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:41.908
our good friend Ice-T,
everybody.

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:43.910
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:53.920 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:00.293 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:00.293 --> 00:01:02.695
How are you?
-What's up, Seth?

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:04.763
-Thank you for spending
your Halloween with us.

00:01:04.763 --> 00:01:07.033
-Well, this is not
Halloween night.

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.434
I'm definitely
gonna go party later.

00:01:08.434 --> 00:01:09.735
-Okay, good.
Yeah, that's --

00:01:09.735 --> 00:01:12.338
For people watching, this is
before the mayhem starts.

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:15.275
-Oh, yeah, this comes on later
than -- when people get home.

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:16.676
-Yeah.
So they'll see you.

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:18.878
Hopefully they all --
when they're watching this,

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:20.679
they should know
that you're still out.

00:01:20.679 --> 00:01:21.814
-Absolutely.
-Yeah.

00:01:21.814 --> 00:01:23.015
[ Laughter ]

00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:24.851
Last year,
you and your wife, Coco,

00:01:24.851 --> 00:01:27.286
were Harley Quinn and the Joker.

00:01:27.286 --> 00:01:29.021
Very well done.
Do you guys have plans?

00:01:29.021 --> 00:01:31.858
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:34.660
-Yeah. Coco's gonna be
this vampire.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.496
You know, whatever she is,
she's gonna be sexy.

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:38.298
-Yeah.
-So a sexy whatever, you know?

00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:39.598
-Right, right, sexy vampire.

00:01:39.598 --> 00:01:41.267
-And then I try
to match that up,

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.069
so I'ma put on a cape
and some teeth and a wig.

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:45.704
-Okay. Seems like
you put less thought into it?

00:01:45.704 --> 00:01:46.739
Is that...?
-Yes.

00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:48.674
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:50.810
-Does -- is this a situation
where you follow her lead?

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.312
Do you ever
come up with it together,

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:54.414
or do you just let her decide?
-I let her decide.

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:56.982
She usually pulls out about four
or five different outfits.

00:01:56.982 --> 00:01:58.451
And then now we have Chanel.
-Yeah.

00:01:58.451 --> 00:01:59.819
-So it's a bunch of costumes

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:01.587
going around the house
during the week.

00:02:01.587 --> 00:02:03.623
And then when she picks
her final one, then I say,

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:05.391
"Okay, I can jump on it."
-Chanel is 6.

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:07.026
What does
she want to be this year?

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:08.495
-She went to school today.

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:10.529
She was a princess, of course.
-Yeah.

00:02:10.529 --> 00:02:12.932
-Something --
they had to do something

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:15.000
to deal with the Renaissance.
-Okay.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.969
-So they had a theme.
And then, tonight,

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:18.537
she's gonna be a vampire
with the rest of us.

00:02:18.537 --> 00:02:19.872
-All right,
so family of vampires.

00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:21.440
There you go.
That's nice.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.676
Do people recognize you when
you're dressed as a vampire?

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:25.812
Do they go, "Oh, my God.
That's Ice-T, the vampire"?

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:28.047
[ Laughter ]

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:29.916
-They definitely recognize Coco.

00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:31.317
-Yeah, that's true.
-They put it together.

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:33.319
Even when we're out, like,
you know, like, if we're out,

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:35.588
if we're at the airport,
somebody, they'll see her,

00:02:35.588 --> 00:02:36.989
and they look,
and then they see me,

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:38.090
they go,
"Oh, I should've known."

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:40.125
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.125 --> 00:02:41.694
When you have the two together,
then it's definite.

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:43.196
-Yeah, exactly.
-Can you tell now --

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:45.464
You've been working on this
television show for twenty --

00:02:45.464 --> 00:02:47.033
I guess, 23 years?
Is that --

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:48.434
'Cause you came in season 2.

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:50.036
-Came in last episode
of the first season.

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:52.638
-Okay, gotcha.
Technically you're in all 24.

00:02:52.638 --> 00:02:54.006
-Yeah, I'm claiming all 24.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.006 --> 00:02:56.909
-Yeah, we'll give you
the full 24.

00:02:56.909 --> 00:02:58.478
But can you tell now
when people come up to you,

00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:00.546
do they -- whether they're gonna
be someone who knows you

00:03:00.546 --> 00:03:01.814
from "SVU"
or someone who knows you

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:03.583
from your hip-hop career,
music career?

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:05.351
-Nah, I can't
tell my fans right now.

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:06.485
-Yeah.
-It's impossible,

00:03:06.485 --> 00:03:08.521
because hip-hop's 50 years old.
-Yeah.

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:10.957
-Hip-hop has gray hair,
so a hip-hop fan

00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:13.259
could be 70 years old.
You could talk to somebody

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:15.561
with a cane, and they're like,
"I used to breakdance."

00:03:15.561 --> 00:03:16.896
[ Laughter ]
You know what I'm saying?

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:19.365
So hip-hop is embedded
in our culture,

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:23.236
so I don't know if I got
a metal fan I'm talking to.

00:03:23.236 --> 00:03:24.504
I don't know
if it's from a film.

00:03:24.504 --> 00:03:26.972
I don't know. But, you know,
I don't really care.

00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:29.608
If you don't like something I do
but you like something else,

00:03:29.608 --> 00:03:31.477
you still like a part of me.
-Yeah.

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:33.312
-So I'm cool with it.
I really don't care.

00:03:33.312 --> 00:03:35.581
A fan is a fan, you know?
-I think one of the things

00:03:35.581 --> 00:03:37.217
about "SVU" --
Absolutely, man.

00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:39.652
[ Cheers and applause ]
Absolutely.

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.387
One of the enduring qualities

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:43.756
about all
the "Law &amp; Order" franchises,

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:45.024
you know,
they rerun all the time.

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:46.592
People enjoy them.
They weirdly --

00:03:46.592 --> 00:03:49.662
Even though -- yeah, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:51.931
That's very comforting to you,
right?

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:54.567
-When I first got on the show,
I was doing movies and stuff,

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:57.503
and I didn't understand
residuals.

00:03:57.503 --> 00:04:00.773
And so the first year,
I did the show, and I'm like,

00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:02.808
"I don't know how long
I'll do this show.

00:04:02.808 --> 00:04:05.043
I don't know how long."
And then I looked --

00:04:05.043 --> 00:04:07.747
My accountant showed me
this number after a year,

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:09.782
and I'm like,
"What's that number?"

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:11.718
And she said,
"That's residuals."

00:04:11.718 --> 00:04:13.353
Now, you don't make residuals
from movies

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:15.355
unless you're, like,
Brad Pitt or somebody.

00:04:15.355 --> 00:04:16.522
-Sure.
-You only make --

00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:18.991
You get like a dollar
for a movie.

00:04:18.991 --> 00:04:21.627
So I saw the number,
and I went from,

00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:23.596
"I don't know how long
I'll be on this show,"

00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:26.232
to, "I will be on this show
till they throw my black ass..."

00:04:26.232 --> 00:04:29.702
[ Laughter ]
"...out in the street."

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:32.571
[ Cheers and applause ]
'Cause we're on Peacock,

00:04:32.571 --> 00:04:36.308
we're on TNT,
we're on now -- right now.

00:04:36.308 --> 00:04:38.611
-Yeah.
Are you upset right now

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:39.745
that somebody's watching this

00:04:39.745 --> 00:04:41.313
when they could be
watching you on "SVU"?

00:04:41.313 --> 00:04:43.416
'Cause you will not be getting
the same residuals from this.

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:45.351
[ Laughter ]

00:04:45.351 --> 00:04:46.919
-You know that "blum blum"?
-Yeah.

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:48.687
-We call that
Dick Wolf's cash register.

00:04:48.687 --> 00:04:53.192
[ Laughter ]
"Blum blum."

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:57.063
-Now, do you ever watch
an old episode of "SVU"?

00:04:57.063 --> 00:04:58.597
-Not really.

00:04:58.597 --> 00:05:01.267
I usually watch the show
when it first comes on.

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:02.869
-Mm-hmm.
-With everybody else.

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:04.170
What people don't understand is,

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.405
we shoot the show
out of sequence.

00:05:06.405 --> 00:05:07.840
I'm not in every scene.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.608
So when they watch it
on television,

00:05:09.608 --> 00:05:12.312
we're watching it for
the first time, too, you know?

00:05:12.312 --> 00:05:14.914
So I'm sitting there like,
"Wow! Yo."

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:16.849
Sometimes I don't
really remember who did it.

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:18.784
I'm like...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:18.784 --> 00:05:20.753
Yo, you know?

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:23.022
Also, we shoot it
out of sequence.

00:05:23.022 --> 00:05:25.758
So we might shoot
the court trial before we even

00:05:25.758 --> 00:05:28.561
figure out who the -- you know,
so it's real jumbled.

00:05:28.561 --> 00:05:30.829
But I can watch it once,
you know.

00:05:30.829 --> 00:05:32.798
Some people walk up to me,
they go, "Yo, I've watched

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:34.567
every episode two times."

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:37.436
I'm like, we might need
to investigate you.

00:05:37.436 --> 00:05:41.240
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.743
It's kind of a dark show.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.743 --> 00:05:47.580
-Do you feel like people --
like, older people use this show

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.982
as a gateway to maybe introduce
their kids to the fact

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:51.583
that you had this hip-hop career

00:05:51.583 --> 00:05:53.853
that a whole generation
might not be aware of?

00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:55.253
-Well, thank God for YouTube.

00:05:55.253 --> 00:05:57.122
And the thing of it is,
all of us that were involved

00:05:57.122 --> 00:05:59.058
with hip-hop,
all the people in the audience,

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:01.861
30, 40, 50, 60,
they believe

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:03.529
that they're cooler
than their kids.

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:05.464
See, that's the one thing
hip-hop did.

00:06:05.464 --> 00:06:06.632
It made you feel like

00:06:06.632 --> 00:06:09.902
your kids are never
gonna be as cool as you.

00:06:09.902 --> 00:06:12.838
So your kid comes in, "Oh,
I like Lil this or Lil that,"

00:06:12.838 --> 00:06:14.840
and you're like,
"Hell no, Run-DMC!

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:17.977
Watch this. This is what
rap looks like," you know?

00:06:17.977 --> 00:06:20.145
And you don't know nothing
about NWA.

00:06:20.145 --> 00:06:25.317
So we -- our generation
pushes our generation

00:06:25.317 --> 00:06:27.586
on the kids 'cause
we are cooler than them.

00:06:27.586 --> 00:06:28.955
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:28.955 --> 00:06:30.923
The fascinating thing, though --

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:32.458
[ Applause ]

00:06:32.458 --> 00:06:34.093
-Got to get your Adidas on,
kids.

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:36.195
You got to understand.
This is what it is.

00:06:36.195 --> 00:06:38.097
-Isn't it funny that when I --
I feel like when my parents

00:06:38.097 --> 00:06:40.466
introduced music to us,
they never had to be like,

00:06:40.466 --> 00:06:43.369
"Oh, no, no, no, not this song!"
because that's --

00:06:43.369 --> 00:06:45.538
with my kids, like, for example
you mentioned NWA,

00:06:45.538 --> 00:06:47.506
and, like, you know, that's --
Again, that's what I grew up,

00:06:47.506 --> 00:06:49.208
when I was driving
to high school and driving home.

00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:50.810
And then I'll say to my kids,
"Oh, you gotta hear this one,"

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.978
and then I'm like,
"What am I doing?"

00:06:52.978 --> 00:06:54.547
-It's okay.
-It's okay?

00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:56.682
We got to push it through?
-'Cause we're cooler.

00:06:56.682 --> 00:06:58.183
-Okay, great.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:58.183 --> 00:07:00.419
I got it from the source.
-It's okay.

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:02.488
-This is your third book,
correct?

00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:04.089
-It's my third book.

00:07:04.089 --> 00:07:06.092
-So this is "Split Decision."

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:07.926
You wrote this with Spike.
Tell us who Spike is.

00:07:07.926 --> 00:07:09.462
-Spike was my crimey.

00:07:09.462 --> 00:07:11.963
Before I got involved in music,
I was in the streets.

00:07:11.963 --> 00:07:13.766
I was breaking the law.
I was doing a lot of things

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:16.702
that were gonna end me up
in the penitentiary.

00:07:16.702 --> 00:07:18.704
And there was a point in my life
where I decided,

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:21.073
you know, "I'm done.
I'm done.

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:22.207
I'm gonna
try to make this music.

00:07:22.207 --> 00:07:23.342
I'ma do something."

00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:25.478
Spike was trying
to be a rapper with me.

00:07:25.478 --> 00:07:27.813
He went and did one more robbery

00:07:27.813 --> 00:07:31.817
and ended up catching
a 36-to-life prison term.

00:07:31.817 --> 00:07:34.954
So when he got out of prison --
he did 26 straight --

00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:36.755
and when he came home, he said,
"I want to write a book."

00:07:36.755 --> 00:07:38.357
I want to write
a cautionary tale

00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:40.392
that tells people
that everything --

00:07:40.392 --> 00:07:44.130
Everybody in this room,
you're one decision away

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:46.665
from basically
destroying your life,

00:07:46.665 --> 00:07:48.667
whether you're getting
in the car with somebody drunk

00:07:48.667 --> 00:07:50.836
or you just --
we make so many decisions,

00:07:50.836 --> 00:07:54.339
and just all it takes
is one bad one.

00:07:54.339 --> 00:07:57.309
And this book right here
shows you my trajectory,

00:07:57.309 --> 00:08:00.079
which I became Ice-T,
getting to sit on your show,

00:08:00.079 --> 00:08:02.914
and what he went through,
and it's a cautionary tale.

00:08:02.914 --> 00:08:04.316
I think everybody
should read it.

00:08:04.316 --> 00:08:06.685
They're gonna find something
in it that's good for them.

00:08:06.685 --> 00:08:07.953
-Well, I really like the fact

00:08:07.953 --> 00:08:09.521
that despite
those different paths,

00:08:09.521 --> 00:08:11.623
it's very nice that you guys
came together to do this.

00:08:11.623 --> 00:08:13.492
-Yes.
-And it's always nice

00:08:13.492 --> 00:08:14.860
to have you here,
sitting on the couch with us.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:16.328
Thank you so much.
-Absolutely.

00:08:16.328 --> 00:08:18.498
-You guys, that's Ice-T.

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:19.932
"Law &amp; Order:
Special Victims Unit"

00:08:19.932 --> 00:08:21.667
airs Thursday nights here on NBC

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:22.935
and streams
the next day on Peacock.

00:08:22.935 --> 00:08:24.871
We'll be right back
with John Irving.

